{
  "summary": "Fix a memory leak when spawning subprocesses on Windows by passing `std::string::data()` directly to `CreateProcessA` instead of heap-allocating a copy. This eliminates an undeleted `new[]` buffer allocation in `Popen::execute_process()`.",
  "problem": "On Windows, `Popen::execute_process()` allocates a command-line buffer on the heap using `new[]` but never deletes it, leaking memory every time a subprocess starts. In Bitcoin Core, this affects Windows nodes configured with `-signer=<cmd>` whenever an external signer process is invoked.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description and commit: \"Popen::execute_process() copied the command line into a buffer from new[], but did not delete it. This leaked memory each time a process started on Windows.\"",
    "PR description: \"This only affects Windows. It is only reachable if -signer=<cmd> is set, through authenticated RPC or the GUI. Each call leaks only the startup-defined <cmd>, and the rate is limited by process spawn time, so the impact is limited.\""
  ],
